  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 313 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Surface high pressure will build into the region\ntoday, bringing drier weather with it. Winds will be gusty out \nof the northwest this morning, then trending lighter as the high\nridges in. Maximum temperatures today will be relatively cool, \ntopping out on the 60s areawide. Tonight frost will be possible \nin the colder valleys of the Adirondacks and Northeast Kingdom. \nSurface ridge will slide east of our region on Tuesday and flow \nwill become southerly and gusty at times. Shortwave energy \nmoving through progressive upper level flow will bring chance \nfor showers Tuesday night into Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A fast progressive flow aloft continues acrs the\nnorthern tier of the CONUS for days 4 thru 7, which results in \nchangeable wx conditions every couple of days. The latest global\nguidance and associated ensemble members still indicate the \nhighest probability of precip (60-75%) is on Thurs, with \nadditional chance (30-40%) pops next weekend. Given the fast \n700-500mb flow of 45 to 60 knots, precip on Thurs wl be quickly \nmoving west to east acrs our cwa, as pw values approach 1.5\". \nGiven the fast flow aloft and limited deep layer instability, \nthe probability of any flooding is very low on Thurs. Next large\nscale system is progged to impact our cwa next weekend with sfc\nlow pres moving along strengthening low lvl thermal gradient. \nGiven time frame, a large spread in timing and placement of \nhighest qpf is noted in the latest guidance. As event becomes \nbetter sampled with time, pops for a portion of next weekend \nwill probably need to be increased, but difficult to determine \nattm. Temps are relatively steady for days 4 thru 7, with \nslightly cooler air possible on Thurs associated with \nclouds/precip and feel latest WPC guidance is probably 3 to 5 \ndegrees too high. Otherwise, expect daily highs in the mid 60s \nmtns to mid 70s valleys with a few spots in the upper 70s \npossible, while lows range from the upper 30s to lower/mid 50s.",
